      "id": 127,
      "title": "Vaccine imprinting drives increased SARS-CoV-2 variant infection in children",
      "url": "https://biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.08.12.739589v1?rss=1",
      "summary": "Virus exposure history, particularly first exposure, is believed to shape vaccine efficacy and infection susceptibility; however, evidence for mechanistic links between immune responses in individuals and epidemiological outcome in populations is scarce. Recent co-circulation of SARS-CoV-2 variants XFG and BA.3.2 has revealed a striking enrichment in BA.3.2 cases among children. By combining epidemiological modeling, serology and monoclonal antibody analysis in children and adults, we show the dependence of effective variant-specific antibodies on vaccination history which may explain birth-year influence on differential susceptibility to these co-circulating variants. Ancestral cross-reactive site I antibodies frequently neutralize BA.3.2, but not XFG. By contrast, Omicron type-specific site I/III and III antibodies frequently neutralize XFG but not BA.3.2, revealing a tradeoff in the ability to neutralize these two co-circulating strains. These findings mechanistically link immune history, variant neutralization, antibody repertoire and variant infection risk, and suggest that vaccination regimens in children should prioritize neutralization breadth.",
      "content": "Vaccine imprinting drives increased SARS-CoV-2 variant infection in children | bioRxiv Skip to main content New Results Vaccine imprinting drives increased SARS-CoV-2 variant infection in children View ORCID Profile Timothy S. Johnston , Rahul Subramanian , Wakinyan Benhamou , Emily Howerton , Robin Schlesinger , Stephen D. Schmidt , Rachel Kazmierski , Mike Castro , Bob C. Lin , Amy R. Henry , Sarah C. Smith , Jesmine Roberts-Torres , Farida Laboune , I-Ting Teng , Shuishu Wang , Dabeluchi Isiofia , Levi Dong , Mark M. Painter , Alicen B. Spaulding , Chaim A. Schramm , David W. Kimberlin , Samuel R. Dominguez , Hai Nguyen-Tran , Perdita Permaul , E. John Wherry , Matthew R. Vogt , Kevin Messacar , Tongqing Zhou , Leonid A. Serebryannyy , Bryan T. Grenfell , Daniel C. Douek doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.08.12.739589 Timothy S. Johnston 1 Vaccine Research Center, NIAID, NIH, Bethesda, MD, USA; Find this author on Google Scholar Find this author on PubMed Search for this author on this site ORCID record for Timothy S. Johnston Rahul Subramanian 1 Vaccine Research Center, NIAID, NIH, Bethesda, MD, USA; Find this author on Google Scholar Find this author on PubMed Search for this author on this site Wakinyan Benhamou 2 Department of Ecology and Evolutionary, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ, USA; Find this author on Google Scholar Find this author on PubMed Search for this author on this site Emily Howerton 2 Department of Ecology and Evolutionary, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ, USA; Find this author on Google Scholar Find this author on PubMed Search for this author on this site Robin Schlesinger 1 Vaccine Research Center, NIAID, NIH, Bethesda, MD, USA; Find this author on Google Scholar Find this author on PubMed Search for this author on this site Stephen D. Schmidt 1 Vaccine Research Center, NIAID, NIH, Bethesda, MD, USA; Find this author on Google Scholar Find this author on PubMed Search for this author on this site Rachel Kazmiersk